deep.reconstruction:
Grothendieck restates an earlier result ('prop. 1 p. 1 de CF V') in an indexed form. CF V is presumably an earlier part of the same manuscript; I am unsure what it is. The purpose is to give a combinatorial axiomatics for 'stratifications' or cell decompositions indexed by a poset, as part of the 1986 Analysis situs notes.

Setting. Let I be a poset and Phi = (X_i)_{i in I} a family of subsets of a set X. The family is implicitly increasing (i <= j implies X_i ⊂ X_j); this was assumed in the previous page's lemma and is used in writing ∂X_i ⊂ X_i. Define:
- the boundary ∂X_i = ∪_{j<i} X_j;
- the open stratum X_i° = X_i \ ∂X_i;
- the support |Phi| = ∪ X_i;
- for each point x, I^x = {i : x ∈ X_i}.

Basic remark. x ∈ X_i° if and only if i is a minimal element of I^x.

The Proposition says the following conditions are equivalent:
- (i) for every x in |Phi|, I^x has a least element;
- (ii) every I^x has a minimal element (automatic if I is finite), and X_i ∩ X_j = ∪_{k<=i,j} X_k for all i, j;
- (iii) (a) the open strata X_i° are pairwise disjoint for i ≠ j, and (b) X_i = ∪_{j<=i} X_j° (automatic if I is finite);
- (iv) with I' = {i : X_i° ≠ ∅}: (α) the X_i°, i ∈ I', partition |Phi|; (β) each X_i is saturated for this partition; (γ) if X_j° ≠ ∅ and X_j° ⊂ X_i, then j <= i.

The proof continues on the next page by splitting 'least element' into three parts: existence of a minimal element, uniqueness of minimal elements, and every element lying above a minimal one.

Modern meaning. Condition (i) says exactly that s: |Phi| → I, x ↦ min I^x, is a well-defined map with X_i = s^{-1}(I_{<=i}) and X_i° = s^{-1}(i). So the proposition characterises which increasing families of subsets ('closed strata') come from a map to the poset, i.e. a poset-stratification in the Alexandrov sense. The open strata are the fibres, and the closed strata are preimages of principal down-sets.

Condition (ii) is the 'meet-preservation' form: the extension D ↦ ∪_{i∈D} X_i from down-sets of I to subsets of X preserves binary intersections. Condition (iv)(γ) says the induced order on strata is no finer than the order on I, which leads to the previous page's iso I → Ĩ.
